#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""
utility-meter — eBus utility-meter publisher reference implementation.

Publishes a single eBus utility-meter device
(`energy.ebus.device.utility-meter`, per `data-models/utility-meter.md`
in the Electrification Bus specification) to an MQTT broker, exposing the
data-model's standard capabilities: `info`, `meter`, `status`, and `doe`.

Intended for developers building an eBus utility-meter publisher who want
a runnable starting point. Suitable for running on a Raspberry Pi or any
Linux host as a stand-in for a real utility meter on a test bench.

The DOE envelopes (`doe/import-limit`, `doe/export-limit`) are two `json`
properties, each a JSON array of time-windowed envelope objects. They are
controllable at runtime via a small HTTP endpoint that stands in for the
utility's out-of-band backhaul:

    POST http://localhost:8765/doe/import-limit
    Content-Type: application/json
    [ { "power-limit": 12000, "source": "GRID",
        "start-time": "2026-07-01T16:00:00Z",
        "end-time":   "2026-07-01T20:00:00Z" } ]

The body is a single envelope object or an array of them (the retained
schedule); the whole array is set atomically as one json value. POST an
empty body (or `null`) to clear the signal for that direction. The same
endpoint shape is served at `/doe/export-limit`.

Meter measurements (`meter/active-power`, etc.) are synthesised from a config
file — this is a publisher reference, not a metrology device.

Usage:
    ./utility-meter --config /path/to/meter-cfg.json

See `utility-meter-cfg.example.json` for the config schema.
"""

